It has also been proposed to use weights for each event within a particular centrality class based on the number of tracks analyzed for that event. The weighted two- and four-particle correlations are then

\[\langle\langle 2\rangle\rangle = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}M_{i}(M_{i}-1).\langle 2\rangle}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}M_{i}(M_{ i}-1)} \tag{3.51}\] \[\langle\langle 4\rangle\rangle = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}M_{i}(M_{i}-1)(M_{i}-2)(M_{i}-3).\langle 4 \rangle}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}M_{i}(M_{i}-1)(M_{i}-2)(M_{i}-3)}, \tag{3.52}\]

and the two and four particle cumulants are calculated from equations 3.51 and 3.52 as

\[c_{n}\{2,QC\} = \langle\langle 2\rangle\rangle \tag{3.53}\] \[c_{n}\{4,QC\} = \langle\langle 4\rangle\rangle-2\langle\langle 2\rangle\rangle^{2} \tag{3.54}\]

This weighting was proposed as a method to reduce the dependence of the results on multiplicity. However, the application of multiplicity weights makes the \(v_{2}\{2,QC\}\) and \(v_{2}\{4,QC\}\) results more dependent on the width of the multiplicity bins used to define centrality.

